EXAMPLES_3 = {
    "ch03-01-from-insights-to-action": """A findings list, prioritized honestly. Say the assessment produced: (a) the loan model leans on a single data point; (b) encryption on the data store is weak; (c) the bias test has never been run. *Threat prioritization:* rank by severity times likelihood — (a) is high-impact and already live, (b) is high-impact but requires an attacker, (c) is high-impact and certain to matter eventually. *Low-hanging fruit first:* (a) is the classic quick win — cheap to fix, visible immediately, and it shows progress, boosts morale, and builds the base for harder risks, exactly as the video argues. *Cost versus benefit* on the rest: security measures are worth it when the cost of reducing the risk is lower than the expected losses. The output is the multi-level plan: strategic considerations at the top, implementation detail at the bottom — never the other way around.""",
    "ch03-02-risk-appetite": """Two organizations, one question. *The startup:* growth is the strategy, capital is thin, risk management is one person — it accepts more risk because its circumstances push it there, and the video says so plainly. *The bank:* strong finances and real risk-management capability, but the strategy is trustworthiness — so it accepts less risk, not because it is more virtuous but because its determinants point that way. The four determinants to cite in every answer: industry, financial strength, strategic goals, and risk management capabilities. The graded insight is that appetite is a *position, not a virtue* — neither end is more correct, and the exercise scores consistency between the profile and the position. [Ch. 23](../../chapters/23-governance-and-standards/README.md) is where the position gets written down as an instrument.""",
    "ch03-03-balancing-innovation-and-security": """The inference runs both ways. *From position to practice:* a company on the innovation end runs agile threat modeling, ships weekly, accepts higher risk, and its security work is built to keep up; a company on the security end runs strict protocols, slower releases, and its security work is built to hold the line. *From practice back to position:* if you observe a company retraining its models on a whim and shipping unreviewed changes, you can infer its position without reading a strategy slide — and predict how it will handle the next identified risk. The rule that survives every stem: there is ALWAYS a compromise between pursuing innovation and ensuring security, and an answer claiming a position escapes the compromise — innovation with no security cost, security with no agility cost — is the marked-wrong one.""",
    "ch03-04-general-landora-implementor": """Landora's first wave, sequenced. The game hands you findings and a budget; the implementor's sequence that wins: (1) take the low-hanging fruit first — the quick wins that show progress and boost morale; (2) price the expensive items by cost versus expected loss; (3) reconcile the stakeholder interests in the plan's wording — security's list, legal's list, the executive's ROI question, collected and reconciled, not competed; (4) publish the multi-level plan, strategic level down to implementation detail. What the game actually grades is alignment, not cleverness: every round scores whether the chosen action serves Landora's stated goals at its stated appetite. A brilliant action misaligned with the strategy loses to a modest action aligned with it — the chapter's title claim (strategic *integration*) made playable.""",
    "ch03-05-aligning-with-organizational-goals": """One decision, both faces. The measure: advanced encryption on customer data. *Face one:* it creates strong safeguards for customer information, supporting a goal of market leadership in customer trust — and it satisfies regulators. *Face two:* the same decision can limit the agility of the AI systems and slow innovation — the agility the strategy also wanted. The video's rule: see both faces BEFORE choosing, because the choice is not "encrypt or not" but "which goal buys the other." The start-up reads the pair one way (open-source tools, flexible, higher accepted risk, security helping rather than hindering innovation); the bank reads it the other way (strict protocols, comprehensive threat modeling, cautious innovation, trust and compliance above speed). Neither reading is wrong; the failure is choosing without seeing the second face.""",
    "ch03-06-connecting-security-with-business-goals": """Four mappings, direction enforced. *Encryption* serves the trust-and-compliance goal — and prices agility. *Agile threat modeling* serves the speed-to-market goal — and prices assurance depth. *Strict access protocols* serve the loyalty-and-regulation goal — and prices innovation speed. *Open-source flexibility* serves the growth goal — and prices control. The exercise's wrong answers reverse the direction: choosing a goal to fit a favorite measure, or mapping a measure to the goal it cannot reach (strict protocols do not create speed). Full credit carries both halves — this measure advances that goal at this price — because a mapping that states the benefit without the cost is a brochure, not an analysis.""",
    "ch03-07-health-care-versus-social-media": """Two sectors, two postures, one procedure. *Health care:* sensitive personal data, safety-critical decisions, heavy regulation — the posture is conservative: minimization on what is collected, strict access, transparency about decisions, security as the foundation of trust. *Social media:* growth and engagement are the strategy, the data volume is the asset, regulation is lighter — the posture leans innovative: rapid iteration, monitoring over gates, higher accepted risk. The procedure that transfers: data sensitivity, decision criticality, regulatory weight, strategy — score any new sector on the four and the posture follows. The exercise brings new industries in later stems; the beginner who can run the scoring can run any sector, which is the point of pairing two opposites first.""",
    "ch03-08-alignment-avenue": """The game's hardest round is the last one: mid-game, the strategy shifts — the growth-first company lands an enterprise contract and must now look like the trust-first company. The losing move is rigidity: keeping the old posture because it won the early rounds. The winning move shifts the security posture with the strategy — stricter protocols, deeper threat modeling, slower releases — and says so in the plan. The avenue is a road you stay ON, not a destination: alignment is a property of the relationship between security and strategy, and when one side moves, the other must move with it. The game's title is its thesis, and its final round grades exactly the move most organizations fail in real life.""",
    "ch03-09-a-security-aware-culture": """One incident, eight catchers. *The incident:* a deployed chatbot begins confidently describing a refund policy that does not exist. *Who catches what:* Operations first (they live in the day-to-day and see the outputs), Data Science next (model health is their duty — is this drift, or a hallucination baseline that was never measured?), Compliance/Legal on the exposure (did we make claims to customers that were false?), IT/Security on the infrastructure (was the model tampered with?), Customer Service on the trust damage (what did users hear, and what do we owe them), HR on the training gap (did the team know hallucination was reportable?), leadership on the tone (do we reward the reporting or shoot the messenger?), and the vendors on the supply-chain question (which dependency fed the bad behavior?). The unified-culture claim, made concrete: the incident is routed to its natural catchers, and nobody says "not my job" — because that phrase is the one failure the culture cannot survive.""",
    "ch03-10-responsible-for-identifying-risks": """Route five risks to their catchers. *"The model's error rate is creeping up month over month."* — Data Science: drift is their line. *"We are not sure we comply with the new data rules."* — Compliance/Legal. *"The AI project duplicates a system we already run."* — Senior leadership: a strategic-risk call about alignment with goals. *"Support tickets mention the assistant making things up."* — Operations and Customer Service jointly: the live-output watchers. *"A library we depend on shipped a suspicious update."* — the vendors under agreed standards, with IT/Security beside them. The exercise's real lesson is the negative space: a risk nobody claims is the culture failure, and the marked-wrong answers are exactly the stems where the risk falls between two chairs.""",
    "ch03-11-role-of-data-science-team-in-security": """The DS team's three duties, separated from the neighbors. *Duty one:* monitor model health and integrity — performance without bias or error over time. *Duty two:* identify drift and unreliable outputs — the symptom-level watch. *Duty three:* identify and correct vulnerabilities in the algorithms that could be exploited. The discrimination drill: *"The API gateway is misconfigured."* — that is IT/Security, not DS: the line is the model and its algorithms. *"We might be non-compliant on retention."* — Compliance/Legal. *"The model is drifting and nobody measured the baseline."* — DS, twice over (the monitoring duty and the missing baseline). The course singles this role out because the DS team owns the model lifecycle — it is the only role that can run chapter 2's monitoring posture inside chapter 3's culture.""",
    "ch03-12-culture-citadel": """The citadel's walls, one round each. *Round one — routing:* the hallucination reports reach the model team in days, not quarters, because Customer Service escalates and Operations corroborates. *Round two — training:* HR's onboarding taught the new analysts that reporting a strange output is a duty, not an alarm. *Round three — tone:* leadership funds the monitoring and rewards the finding, so the reports keep coming. *Round four — partners:* the vendors ship fixes under the agreed standards, and the supply chain holds. The game grades the routing, not the heroics — one role cannot hold the citadel, and the losing rounds are always culture failures: a risk everyone assumed someone else owned, a tone that treats security as a tech-team matter. The video's thesis, playable: unified culture is the structure that keeps monitoring and protection running over time.""",
    "ch03-13-ai-security-for-the-future": """Three future topics, one posture. *Regulation shifts:* new rules on data collection land — the adaptive organization has been watching both government and public sentiment, so the change is absorbed, not survived; the reactive organization treats the same rule as an ambush. *Synthetic data arrives:* artificial data sets that resemble real information without personal details ease the privacy concern — and immediately introduce their own integrity challenge: measures must be designed for the robustness of synthetic data, not just for its convenience. *AGI is debated:* the experts disagree about likelihood, and the course teaches the disagreement as the fact — awareness of the possibility, with robust security considerations built in from the beginning if it arrives. The posture that answers all three: explainability and transparency (quicker adaptation as threats arise), continuous improvement (outdated defenses are the vulnerability), and adaptive leadership (proactive strategy instead of reaction). The repo's [Ch. 23](../../chapters/23-governance-and-standards/README.md) is the regulation half at full depth.""",
    "ch03-14-staying-up-to-date": """Four needs, four channels. *"Earliest possible signal on a new attack pattern."* — security researchers' work: the warnings arrive before the publications do. *"A structured, curated record of the field."* — the relevant publications: slow, reliable, citable. *"A read on where the practice is heading this year."* — industry conferences: the state of the art, in person. *"Is anyone else seeing what we are seeing?"* — the community and the online forums: verification by peers. The wrong-answer engine is stagnation: outdated defenses are more vulnerable to attacks, data breaches, and compliance issues — eroding trust, costing money, hindering innovation — and any stem where an organization stops updating and expects security to hold is the marked failure. The maintenance posture is a habit, not a purchase; that is what this exercise drills.""",
    "ch03-15-it-takes-a-village": """Run each future topic through the four pillars. *Synthetic data:* people (who validates it?), legal (what may it claim to resemble?), technology (what does it change in the pipeline?), processes (what must be re-verified before training on it?). *A regulation shift:* people (who tracks it?), legal (what does it require?), technology (what must change?), processes (what updates and when?). *AGI debates:* people (whose judgment do we trust, and on what record?), legal (what would governance even require?), technology (what would robust-from-the-beginning mean?), processes (what would continuous improvement look like at that scale?). The exercise's rule: future topics are ecosystem topics — route each through the pillars rather than treating it as a technical curiosity, and never assign the future to one team. The village, in the future tense, is the same village.""",
    "ch03-16-throne-of-stagnation": """Score one hold against one adapt. *The change:* a new class of prompt-based attacks begins circulating. *The hold:* "our filters were fine last year" — and the game prices it: the accumulated costs arrive together (vulnerability, breach exposure, compliance drift, trust erosion), which is the argument the entire course has been building. *The adapt:* threat modeling updated this quarter, researchers' warnings in the weekly reading, filters revised, and the trade-off price stated honestly (some agility spent). The throne is the trap in the title: stagnation feels like stability — nothing changes, nothing breaks, nothing needs deciding — and it prices like fragility. The winning line is the video's closing claim made playable: adaptive leadership, proactive strategy, security in motion.""",
    "ch03-17-wrap-up-ai-security-and-risk-management": """The exit, planned. The course's own recap: chapter 1 gave the five AI-specific risks and the ecosystem; chapter 2 the model risks, external threats, and assessment; chapter 3 the strategic integration — the start-up-versus-bank contrast as the emblem. The instructor's stated follow-ons: AI Ethics, AI Strategy, Explainable AI Concepts, and Introduction to Data Security. From here, the honest ladder into this repository: the survey courses open the vocabulary; [Ch. 01](../../chapters/01-risk-models-and-research-epistemics/README.md) teaches the same taxonomy with the evidence discipline behind it; Ch. 02 turns leakage and overfitting into measurement; Ch. 12–16 take the attack surface to full depth; Ch. 22–23 build the assessment posture into documents and instruments; and the careers index maps the routes the follow-ons gesture at, with entry mechanisms and progress ladders. The survey is the invitation; the chapters are the work.""",
}
